---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import MCQuiz from '../../components/MCQuiz.astro';
---

<Layout title="params.require vs params[]: what's the failure mode?" description="Practice card. Two ways to reach into params; two different errors when the key is missing. Drills strong parameters.">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Course
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Practice · Reading the Source · Card 3</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4"><code class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 rounded text-3xl">require</code> vs <code class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 rounded text-3xl">[]</code>: what's the failure mode?</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">Two ways to reach into params for the same data. When the form submits without the expected key, they fail differently. The difference matters.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-gray-500">🔍</span> The code
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 italic mb-3">A POST comes in with no <code>post</code> key in the body.</p>
          <div class="border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-gray-50/40">
            <CodeBlock code={`# Version 1:
params.require(:post).permit(:title, :body)

# Version 2:
params[:post].permit(:title, :body)`} language="ruby" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">The question</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Both versions fail when the <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">post</code> key is missing. Which pair of errors do they raise?</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <MCQuiz
          options={[
            {
              label: 'A',
              text: 'Both raise <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">ActionController::ParameterMissing</code>.',
              explanation: 'Only <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">require</code> raises that specific error. The bracket version doesn\'t know the key was supposed to be there.',
            },
            {
              label: 'B',
              text: 'Version 1 raises <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">ActionController::ParameterMissing</code> (400). Version 2 raises <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">NoMethodError</code> on nil (500).',
              explanation: '<code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">require(:post)</code> asserts the key exists and tells Rails to render a 400 Bad Request automatically. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">params[:post]</code> returns nil, and calling <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">.permit</code> on nil hits <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">NoMethodError</code>, which Rails surfaces as a 500.',
              correct: true,
            },
            {
              label: 'C',
              text: 'Version 1 returns an empty Parameters object. Version 2 raises <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">NoMethodError</code>.',
              explanation: '<code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">require</code> never returns silently for a missing key. That\'s its whole job.',
            },
            {
              label: 'D',
              text: 'Both raise <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">NoMethodError</code>.',
              explanation: '<code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">require</code> raises its own specific error before any chained method would have the chance to fail.',
            },
          ]}
          principle={`<p>A missing form key is a client problem (they sent bad input), not a server crash. It should be a 400, not a 500. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">require</code> turns the failure into a 400 with a clear message that names the missing key. The bracket version produces a 500 that wakes someone up at 2 AM and tells them their controller has a NoMethodError on nil.</p><p>Use <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">require</code>. It\'s the Rails idiom for a reason.</p>`}
          theoryHref="/lessons/source-params"
          theoryLabel="Reading the Source · Card 5 — How params works (and what permit does)"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/lessons/practice-source-when-sql-runs" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Card 2</a>
      <a href="/lessons/practice-source-before-action-halt" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">Card 4 →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
